From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:26:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 185/294] gallium/pvr: the kernel now CREATES the free lists
 and HWRT data set

Getting there took four corrections, each one the kernel naming the rule I had broken:

 1. free-list page counts are not free: CREATE_FREE_LIST wants the device's own
    free_list_min_pages..max_pages (25..524288 here), not a guessed range -> queried, not assumed.
 2. a free-list buffer holds ROGUE_FREE_LIST_ENTRY_SIZE (4) bytes per PM page, not a page per page.
 3. the UAPI states the rest explicitly: counts must be multiples of 4, and with grow_num_pages == 0
    the initial count must EQUAL the maximum. Also grow_threshold is a percentage, not a page count.
 4. the free-list BO must be created with DRM_PVR_BO_PM_FW_PROTECT and WITHOUT userspace CPU access -
    so pvr_bo_create_flags() exists now and the old helper is a wrapper over it.

Result against the real kernel:

  hwrt: free lists 1/2 and HWRT data set 1 CREATED for 512x512

Both free lists and the data set are accepted. The render job's submit then moves from
"Invalid argument" (no hwrt) to "Bad address" (EFAULT), i.e. the job description is now structurally
valid and the kernel objects to an address instead. Filling rt_data[1] and the RTC buffer did not
change it, so the next suspect is the command stream BO's own mapping/flags rather than the dataset.

Also fixed a SIGSEGV I introduced: bos[10]/ads[10] were given 11 initialisers while the loop ran to 11,
writing past the arrays. The probe runs clean again under the shim (exit 0, ALL PASS).
